
What is Queue interface ?
  --> 1️⃣ Queue Interface in Java

      Package: java.util.Queue

      Type: Interface (implemented by LinkedList, PriorityQueue, ArrayDeque, etc.)

      Purpose: FIFO (First In First Out) data structure

      Hierarchy:

      java.lang.Object
         ↳ java.util.AbstractCollection<E>
             ↳ java.util.Queue<E>

  | Method       | Description             | Throws Exception                  | Special Behavior           |
  | ------------ | ----------------------- | --------------------------------- | -------------------------- |
  | `add(E e)`   | Inserts element at tail | `IllegalStateException` if full   | Throws exception if failed |
  | `offer(E e)` | Inserts element at tail | No exception                      | Returns `false` if failed  |
  | `remove()`   | Removes head element    | `NoSuchElementException` if empty | Throws exception if empty  |
  | `poll()`     | Removes head element    | No exception                      | Returns `null` if empty    |
  | `element()`  | Retrieves head element  | `NoSuchElementException` if empty | Throws exception if empty  |
  | `peek()`     | Retrieves head element  | No exception                      | Returns `null` if empty    |


3️⃣ Common Implementations
  Class	                     Characteristics
LinkedList	            Doubly linked list, allows null, implements Queue & Deque
PriorityQueue	        Orders elements according to natural ordering(Comparable) or Comparator
ArrayDeque	            Resizable array, faster than LinkedList, implements Queue & Deque
ConcurrentLinkedQueue	Thread-safe, fail-safe iterator


4️⃣ Fail-Fast vs Fail-Safe Iterators
Fail-Fast: LinkedList, ArrayDeque → throws ConcurrentModificationException if modified during iteration
Fail-Safe: ConcurrentLinkedQueue → does not throw exception; iterator works on a snapshot